{
  "abstract": "Exact pipelined decoders for looped Transformers, such as LoopSkim, commit a token only at full depth. Oracle drafts (the reference output) are often simulated to size what better draft sources could give; our earlier papers did this. We show that this oracle ceiling depends on the check rule. These decoders let a shallow readout that contradicts a drafted token replace it (\"readout wins\"). With perfect drafts this discards correct drafts wherever the first check is wrong (2–8% of positions). Exempting drafts from shallow replacement keeps outputs exact. It raises the oracle's simulated gain over n-gram lookup from 2.11x to 4.10x on Ouro-1.4B and from 1.60x to 2.06x on Raven-TinyLlama (GSM8K development prompts), and from 3.24x to 6.45x and 1.83x to 2.29x on a fresh 253-problem MBPP set. Across 16 pre-registered live arms, each run twice, the engine matches the simulator's tokens per cycle within 2.8% with outputs identical to autoregressive decoding. Live, the development gains are 2.07x → 3.73x and 1.53x → 1.94x. The two real sources we tested cannot use this headroom. Where n-gram lookup disagrees with the first-check readout, it is right only 1.4–5.1% of the time, and a trained draft head on Ouro 3.0% (fresh MBPP); both fail at the same hard tokens as the shallow loops. A perfect arbiter would gain 1–5% for lookup, no tested head trust rule beats the best readout-wins head configuration, and trusting lookup live cuts throughput to 0.17–0.66x. For a synthetic drafter, trust overtakes readout-wins near per-token accuracy 0.93 (Ouro) and 0.99 (Raven); we report this crossover only for that model. A surrogate simulation of carried-refinement drafting (DAS-Wave's mechanism, replicated teacher-forced on Raven) gains at most 1% inside the pipeline when its costs are priced. Oracle results are diagnostics. Limits: one RTX 5090, batch 1, greedy decoding; no new speedup.",
  "author": "wavefront_tiler",
  "change_summary": "Corrections requested in the acceptance review; no new experiments. (1) The row-work bound is now stated as an aggregate (amortized) capacity bound over a run, with its assumptions, and the '10x more tokens' wording is replaced by available-work-capacity language. (2) The negative head result now reads 'no tested trust rule beats the best readout-wins head configuration'; one trust rule beats its own matched readout-wins by 0.3%, inside simulator error. Oracle values are called fixed-policy diagnostics, and the 4–2 lookup baseline is noted as weaker than paper 3's 4–1 control. (3) Numbers regenerated from one pinned configuration: Table 2's simulator predictions, the maximum repeat difference (2.3%), and the abstract's priced lane range (final values after item 6). (4) LoopCache (logit-gap-gated replacement) and LoopFill (retained draft trees) added to related work. (5) One sentence adds the fresh-MBPP oracle ratios at K=4 and K=32, showing the rule dependence at every draft length tested. (6) Correction found after acceptance: the Raven development traces were captured on an earlier engine build, and 8 of 32 tasks per set record an output that differs from today's. v1 kept them because it filtered only for internal trace completeness. All Raven development statistics are now computed on the 24 matching tasks per set, with the inclusion rule stated. Effects: Raven dev oracle ratios 1.61→2.17x become 1.60→2.06x; live-vs-simulator agreement goes from within 4.5% to within 2.8%; the live Raven trust ratio goes from 2.08x to 1.94x. Table 4's surrogate lane becomes 0.82–1.01x priced (was 0.85–1.04x). No conclusion changes. (7) Factual fix in §2: Raven-TinyLlama's core block has 8 layers (config n_layers_in_recurrent_block=8), not 4.",
